ans  we don't employ child soldiers with boys  who are eight nine and ten years old  that's what the houthis do  the houthis stop humanitarian assistance  from reaching people they stop  the world fruit program from  distributing food they stop the world  health organization from  distributing cholera vaccine they robbed  the central bank they wrapped the  phone company they robbed the income  from the port of fredera  and yet nobody seems to be blaming them  it's tragic that this war has gone on  for so long  yemen is our neighbor we want yemen to  prosper and to be stable  the war in afghanistan took the united  states and its coalition partners 19  years and it's still not over  the war against daesh in syria and iraq  started  involves more than 70 of the world's  greatest powers and it's been going on  now for five years or six years  and it hasn't ended yet so i'm not  saying  that the war in yemen that that minister  you saying that this war i mean  you're equating it to afghanistan are  you saying this one could go on for many  many more years  i hope not and we're trying our best to  try to put it to an end very quickly  back to the g20 a very exciting moment  for saudi arabia and a moment of hope  and  looking forward to the future but at the  same time  it is clear from what we've heard over  the past few days that that human rights  issues are  uh still at the fore when when it comes  to saudi arabia i want to talk briefly  about lujanat  al al-hathoul she is still in prison  when will she be released and why is she  in prison in the first place  she will be released when the court when  the legal process plays out  for decisions being her case is being  reviewed by the courts  and then once she's released then she  will either be punished or she'll be  released it's up to the courts  the uh the decision to uh  detain her had to do with national  security shares and not with the  advocating the right to women to drive  which every woman in saudi arabia  